Subclinical Hashimoto's Thyroiditis
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Elevated TPO antibodies, mild unrefreshing sleep, subtle weight changes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-044
Reason for Visit: Acute severe health anxiety and palpitations following the unguided review of abnormal pathology results, requiring exclusion of acute cardiac or metabolic derangement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute subjective palpitations, shortness of breath, and severe distress in a patient who recently received abnormal thyroid blood results. Immediate clinical review was necessary to exclude a true cardiac arrhythmia or acute hypoxic event prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I read my test results and it says my thyroid is failing. I feel incredibly weak, I can't catch my breath, and my heart is skipping beats. I'm terrified my body is shutting down.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an incidental finding of elevated TSH with normal T4 on recent screening. Following online self-research an hour ago, the patient developed an acute, severe sympathetic nervous system response, presenting with sudden tachycardia, hyperventilation, and generalized weakness. Patient reports a history of mild unrefreshing sleep and subtle weight changes but was otherwise physically stable prior to reading the results. Denies chest pain, syncope, or true orthopnea.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High health anxiety driving acute physiological distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, hyperventilating slightly, visibly tense.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a rapid, regular rhythm without murmurs or rubs. No dropped beats or irregular rhythm appreciated during 60 seconds of auscultation. Respiratory exam is clear to auscultation bilaterally with good air entry. Thyroid gland is not palpably enlarged or tender. Neurological exam reveals normal, symmetrical reflexes without delayed relaxation phase. History confirms elevated TPO antibodies, mild unrefreshing sleep, subtle weight changes.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment, computer open to medical search results.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current thyroid replacement therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Anxiety Response secondary to incidental Subclinical Hashimoto's Thyroiditis findings - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Regular sinus tachycardia clearly correlating with acute panic, and absence of irregularly irregular rhythm or syncope.

Myxedema Coma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal mentation, normal body temperature, absence of bradycardia, and known normal T4 levels.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal oxygen saturation on room air, clear lungs, and absence of pleuritic chest pain or DVT risk factors.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute autonomic overactivity, subjective palpitations, and dyspnea secondary to misinterpreting subclinical autoimmune pathology results. Triage appropriately mandated immediate physical evaluation to rule out a true cardiac arrhythmia or respiratory compromise. Clinical examination firmly established normal hemodynamics, a regular heart rhythm, and the complete absence of acute metabolic or cardiac pathology. The rapid provision of clinical reassurance and explanation of the benign, early-stage diagnosis successfully aborted a severe panic attack, entirely preventing an unnecessary emergency department surge.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the non-acute, long-term nature of subclinical thyroiditis. Guided box-breathing techniques utilized to slow respiratory rate.
Positive Behavioral Change: Close all internet browser tabs related to medical research immediately and practice the '5-4-3-2-1' grounding technique. By actively redirecting your focus away from medical anxiety and into your physical surroundings, your breathing will normalize and the palpitations will stop within minutes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment next week to discuss long-term thyroid monitoring and potential symptomatic management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe, central chest pain, if your heart begins beating erratically and irregularly, or if you feel faint and lose consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal endocrine review; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Subclinical Hashimoto's means your thyroid is working slightly harder to maintain normal hormone levels. It is a very slow-moving condition. Your body is not shutting down tonight.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if your shortness of breath persists even when you are completely calm and resting, or if you develop significant swelling in your lower legs.

